-- title:
--   Every cusp place of X₀(N) arises from a slot
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field containing the rationals, $N$ a nonzero natural number, and $\zeta$ a unit of $K$ whose underlying element is a primitive $N$th root of unity. Let $F =$ `laurentBaseChange K (modularFunctionFieldFull N)`, i.e. the subfield of the Laurent series field $K((q))$ generated over $K$ by the coefficientwise images under $\mathrm{alg}_{\mathbb{Q}\to K}$ of the subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated by the series $j(q^{d})$ for the nonzero divisors $d$ of $N$, and let $w$ be a place of $F$ over $K$ (a valuation subring of $F$ containing $K$, not all of $F$, and a principal ideal ring). Assume the element $j(q)$ of $F$ is not in the valuation subring of $w$, i.e. $j$ has a pole at $w$. Then there are natural numbers $a, b$ with $a \mid N$, $a \neq 0$, $b < N/a$ and $\gcd(\gcd(a,b), N/a) = 1$, together with a $K$-algebra homomorphism $\iota : F \to K((q))$ such that $\iota(j(q)) = j(q^{N})$, $\iota(j(q^{N}))$ is the series obtained from $j(q)$ by the coefficient twist $c_k \mapsto \zeta^{bak} c_k$ followed by substitution $q \mapsto q^{a^{2}}$, and, for every $x \in F$, $\operatorname{ord}_w(x) \cdot (a\gcd(a, N/a)) = \operatorname{order}(\iota x)$, the order of the Laurent series $\iota x$.
--
--   This is the statement that the places of the function field of $X_0(N)$ at which $j$ has a pole are exactly the cusps, each obtained from one of the slots $(a,b)$ with $a \mid N$, $b < N/a$ and $\gcd(\gcd(a,b),N/a)=1$, the factor $a\gcd(a,N/a)$ being the width of the corresponding cusp. It is used in the analysis of the characteristic-$p$ fibre models, in particular in the divisibility statements for the order of $j$ at a place lying over a cusp.
